      "text": "1. To find the month when Hal will change the batteries for the 25th time, we need to determine the pattern in which the batteries are changed and then calculate the 25th change.\n2. We know that the batteries are changed every 5 months.\n3. We can use the formula for the n-th term of an arithmetic sequence to find the month.\n   \\[\n   a_n = a_1 + (n-1)d\n   \\]\n   where \\(a_n\\) is the number of the term, \\(a_1\\) is the first term, \\(n\\) is the term number, and \\(d\\) is the common difference.\n4. In this problem, \\(a_1 = 5\\) (since the first change is in May, which is the 5th month), \\(n = 25\\) (since we need to find the 25th change), and \\(d = 5\\) (since the change is every 5 months).\n5. Plugging these values into the formula, we get:\n   \\[\n   a_{25} = 5 + (25-1) \\cdot 5\n   \\]\n6. Simplify the expression inside the parentheses:\n   \\[\n   a_{25} = 5 + 24 \\cdot 5\n   \\]\n7. Multiply 24 by 5:\n   \\[\n   a_{25} = 5 + 120\n   \\]\n8. Add the numbers together:\n   \\[\n   a_{25} = 125\n   \\]\n9. The number 125 corresponds to the month. To find the month, we need to determine the position of 125 in the sequence of months starting from May (which is the 5th month). We subtract 5 from 125:\n   \\[\n   125 - 5 = 120\n   \\]\n10. The month number 120 in a common year means it is June (since the months are ordered as January, February, March, April, May, June, July, August, September, October, November, December in a 12-month cycle).\n\nConclusion: \n\\[\n\\boxed{June}\n\\]",
